WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:02.870
- I'm in, like, the
biggest heel right now.

00:00:02.870 --> 00:00:04.290
Usually I'm here.

00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:05.310
That's amazing!
- I'm a giant.

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:07.550
- I look like an Oompa Loompa!

00:00:07.550 --> 00:00:10.120
Oh my gosh, it's fine, it's fine.

00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:11.420
I'm cheating in my heels.

00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:12.253
How are you?

00:00:12.253 --> 00:00:13.086
Have ya'll met before?

00:00:13.086 --> 00:00:15.330
- This young man I think
was on the Cardinals.

00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:17.177
- Yes I was.

00:00:17.177 --> 00:00:18.323
- I'm from Arizona so,

00:00:18.323 --> 00:00:19.703
of course I love it.

00:00:19.703 --> 00:00:21.210
I know who you are.

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:22.700
This guy's great so,

00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:24.397
don't just take her
word for it, take mine.

00:00:24.397 --> 00:00:25.850
(laughing)

00:00:25.850 --> 00:00:27.660
- Well Calais how does
it feel to receive one

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:29.100
of the most coveted sports awards?

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:29.940
That's pretty amazing.

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:31.348
- Uh yeah. It's been incredible.

00:00:31.348 --> 00:00:33.020
You know I think the
biggest thing is I'm more

00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:33.853
inspired than ever, you know?

00:00:33.853 --> 00:00:36.357
It's gave me a lot of
motivation to want to do more.

00:00:37.294 --> 00:00:38.127
It's pretty cool.

00:00:38.127 --> 00:00:38.960
- And you're doing amazing things.

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:40.910
I love when people have
such a big spotlight.

00:00:40.910 --> 00:00:42.610
Especially sports figures
because a lot of little

00:00:42.610 --> 00:00:44.281
kids look up to y'all,
and the fact that you use

00:00:44.281 --> 00:00:47.100
it for such good its
like you're super heroes.

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:48.058
Its really cool.

00:00:48.058 --> 00:00:49.143
- Thank you.

00:00:49.143 --> 00:00:50.374
I take pride in being a role model.

00:00:50.374 --> 00:00:51.207
I really do.

00:00:51.207 --> 00:00:52.630
- Yeah that's awesome, that's awesome.

00:00:52.630 --> 00:00:53.940
A lot of people see it as a burden

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:55.000
so its awesome that you take pride in it

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:56.470
and you think about it positively, but

00:00:56.470 --> 00:00:58.460
David, what is the best
award you've ever won?

00:00:58.460 --> 00:00:59.340
What do you think?

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:01.024
- Kelly, I'm not really an awards guy.

00:01:01.024 --> 00:01:02.340
(laughing)

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:05.380
I think its just nice
to be almost nominated.

00:01:05.380 --> 00:01:06.213
- Almost!

00:01:06.213 --> 00:01:07.440
(laughing)

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:09.650
- Now, I was up for a
Golden Globe and an Emmy.

00:01:09.650 --> 00:01:10.483
- [Kelly] Wow!

00:01:10.483 --> 00:01:11.316
- It went the wrong way at

00:01:11.316 --> 00:01:13.180
the last minute when they announced it.

00:01:13.180 --> 00:01:14.560
But it's exciting.

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:15.623
No, it's great.

00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:18.334
- So Calais have you always
had such a giving spirit?

00:01:18.334 --> 00:01:20.260
Where did it come from?

00:01:20.260 --> 00:01:21.168
- It started with my family.

00:01:21.168 --> 00:01:22.712
You know growing up, my parents you know,

00:01:22.712 --> 00:01:24.490
especially my dad.

00:01:24.490 --> 00:01:26.441
He really installed it
in us at a young age,

00:01:26.441 --> 00:01:29.290
you have to be involved in
the community, giving back.

00:01:29.290 --> 00:01:31.370
Even if you don't have a
lot to give back, give time.

00:01:31.370 --> 00:01:33.750
We used to push cars out of the snow

00:01:33.750 --> 00:01:35.940
and like, go help.

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:38.010
My dad used to make me
go out and help ladies

00:01:38.010 --> 00:01:39.034
with their groceries and stuff.

00:01:39.034 --> 00:01:40.570
- Yeah, he was raising a gentleman.

00:01:40.570 --> 00:01:42.360
- Yeah you know, and
it really has paid off,

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:46.120
'cause I really am
inspired and I try to do,

00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:46.953
I feel like the only thing you can

00:01:46.953 --> 00:01:48.170
really control is your effort,

00:01:48.170 --> 00:01:49.980
and I don't know if I'll make a difference

00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:51.310
in this world, but I'm
definitely gonna try.

00:01:51.310 --> 00:01:53.841
- Yeah, and hey, I'm like
(applause)

00:01:53.841 --> 00:01:54.703
a firm believer, I'm a firm believer.

00:01:56.320 --> 00:01:58.709
I'm a church kid, so even if you help

00:01:58.709 --> 00:02:00.161
one person it's worth it.

00:02:00.161 --> 00:02:00.994
- [Calais] Oh yeah.

00:02:00.994 --> 00:02:01.870
- You know, and you're
helping way more than that.

00:02:01.870 --> 00:02:04.330
But even if it is just one
you're still a super hero

00:02:04.330 --> 00:02:05.234
if you're helping someone.

00:02:05.234 --> 00:02:07.830
Tell us about your CRC Foundation.

00:02:07.830 --> 00:02:10.140
- I started it with my family.

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:11.600
A bunch of my brothers and sisters are,

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:13.440
I have seven siblings, and--

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:14.273
- Wow!

00:02:14.273 --> 00:02:15.106
Your mom.

00:02:15.106 --> 00:02:15.939
Okay. (laughs)

00:02:15.939 --> 00:02:16.772
- She's an incredible woman.

00:02:16.772 --> 00:02:18.173
- She is a mighty one!

00:02:19.030 --> 00:02:20.810
- But we just wanted to do something

00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:23.220
for my dad's legacy, and I got to the NFL

00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:24.400
and I saw this platform,

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:27.227
and just this ability to be
able to help so many people,

00:02:27.227 --> 00:02:30.370
and so we tried to just
find different ways

00:02:30.370 --> 00:02:31.740
to help so many kids, and it really

00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:34.810
comes down to just, you
know, spending time.

00:02:34.810 --> 00:02:37.710
You know I try to, I'm a
church-going person as well

00:02:37.710 --> 00:02:39.083
and I try to say that I'm gonna

00:02:39.083 --> 00:02:41.260
give 10% of my time to charity,

00:02:41.260 --> 00:02:43.150
and to giving back and
making a difference.

00:02:43.150 --> 00:02:43.983
And that's all I can do sometimes.

00:02:43.983 --> 00:02:45.130
- Yeah, like tithing, yeah, yeah.

00:02:45.130 --> 00:02:47.790
- But it is definitely
something that is special to me.

00:02:47.790 --> 00:02:50.430
- The best kind of human,
I think, is consistent.

00:02:50.430 --> 00:02:52.670
And that's because the
worst kind, I feel like,

00:02:52.670 --> 00:02:53.870
is an inconsistent human,

00:02:53.870 --> 00:02:56.440
and I think especially
when you help with kids,

00:02:56.440 --> 00:02:57.830
like that's the coolest
thing that you're still

00:02:57.830 --> 00:02:59.610
constantly kind of
appearing in their lives,

00:02:59.610 --> 00:03:02.061
and not going away so thank you for that.

00:03:02.061 --> 00:03:02.894
- My pleasure.

00:03:02.894 --> 00:03:05.670
- But what are some things
the CRC Foundation does?

00:03:05.670 --> 00:03:06.863
Like how do they help?

00:03:06.863 --> 00:03:09.470
- Well, we do a lot, but I think

00:03:09.470 --> 00:03:12.970
the things that we really
try to focus on is really,

00:03:12.970 --> 00:03:15.110
so, when I was younger,
I was in seventh grade,

00:03:15.110 --> 00:03:16.785
and I really didn't enjoy reading.

00:03:16.785 --> 00:03:18.322
You know, I read 'cause I had to.

00:03:18.322 --> 00:03:20.082
And then I had a teacher challenge me,

00:03:20.082 --> 00:03:21.222
and like put a book in front of me.

00:03:21.222 --> 00:03:22.983
And that really connected with me, and

00:03:22.983 --> 00:03:24.050
then I started reading all the time.

00:03:24.050 --> 00:03:26.030
I loved reading from that point on.

00:03:26.030 --> 00:03:28.950
So now my biggest goal is to really just

00:03:28.950 --> 00:03:29.960
get kids involved in reading,

00:03:29.960 --> 00:03:31.430
so I created reading programs,

00:03:31.430 --> 00:03:33.190
and I'm just trying to
find ways to make reading

00:03:33.190 --> 00:03:34.547
more fun for a lot of the kids.

00:03:34.547 --> 00:03:36.710
- Yeah, but last season you created the

00:03:36.710 --> 00:03:38.730
Season of Giving what is that about?

00:03:38.730 --> 00:03:40.490
- Because of this great job I have,

00:03:40.490 --> 00:03:42.720
you've got this big platform.

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:44.120
And there are a lot of
charities that I like,

00:03:44.120 --> 00:03:47.990
and I wanted to really
help bring exposure,

00:03:47.990 --> 00:03:49.280
some awareness to some charities

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:50.760
that really meant a lot to me.

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:54.350
So I created this Season of Giving.

00:03:54.350 --> 00:03:57.230
I'm gonna take $100,000 I want to give

00:03:57.230 --> 00:03:58.670
so I went in and looked
at my stats for the last

00:03:58.670 --> 00:04:00.480
five years and I broke it
down, and I was like okay,

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:01.593
I'm gonna give 5,000 for a sack,

00:04:01.593 --> 00:04:04.740
give 10,000 for like a
touchdown or a big turnover.

00:04:04.740 --> 00:04:05.573
- Yeah. That's cool.

00:04:05.573 --> 00:04:07.584
- Give 1,000 for just winning the game.

00:04:07.584 --> 00:04:10.880
So my goal was to give
20,000 to each charity,

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:12.780
and so I did four different charities.

00:04:12.780 --> 00:04:14.050
And then when I was in
Denver I broke it down to

00:04:14.050 --> 00:04:15.426
four charities from where I'm from

00:04:15.426 --> 00:04:17.740
'cause we were playing in
Denver, which was incredible.

00:04:17.740 --> 00:04:18.940
- What?
- We won the game and it

00:04:18.940 --> 00:04:20.360
was awesome it was a lot of fun.

00:04:20.360 --> 00:04:21.977
- Are you ever like, mmm,
if I do that I'll owe,

00:04:21.977 --> 00:04:23.477
mmm I'll leave that one there.

00:04:24.990 --> 00:04:25.823
(laughing)

00:04:25.823 --> 00:04:26.656
- It's amazing though and it makes it

00:04:26.656 --> 00:04:27.770
fun too for like the kids watching,

00:04:27.770 --> 00:04:28.830
and the families watching.

00:04:28.830 --> 00:04:30.427
Like "Oh my God. That's
two grand right there!"

00:04:30.427 --> 00:04:31.450
That's pretty cool.

00:04:31.450 --> 00:04:33.210
- And the people want to
give a lot along with me too.

00:04:33.210 --> 00:04:35.280
So it was like they
could donate too through

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:36.390
the website we had.

00:04:36.390 --> 00:04:37.535
- Ah, what a clever idea!

00:04:37.535 --> 00:04:38.368
- It was pretty cool.

00:04:38.368 --> 00:04:39.260
- Yeah, all right!

00:04:39.260 --> 00:04:41.010
So Calais, your foundation also gives out

00:04:41.010 --> 00:04:42.142
scholarships I hear?

00:04:42.142 --> 00:04:42.975
- This is true.

00:04:42.975 --> 00:04:44.820
- This is awesome 'cause
like that's a huge thing.

00:04:44.820 --> 00:04:46.047
Rackin' up all that debt
for college students.

00:04:46.047 --> 00:04:47.140
That's really cool.

00:04:47.140 --> 00:04:48.807
- Yeah, I mean, I think that

00:04:48.807 --> 00:04:50.850
I want to inspire people and help people

00:04:50.850 --> 00:04:52.650
like that to actually have potential to do

00:04:52.650 --> 00:04:53.483
something with their lives,

00:04:53.483 --> 00:04:55.840
and it really started out helping people

00:04:55.840 --> 00:04:57.106
come from similar backgrounds as me.

00:04:57.106 --> 00:04:57.939
- Yeah.

00:04:57.939 --> 00:05:01.120
- Big families, financially
stressful and need help.

00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:04.080
And so I've given away a
few scholarships in my day.

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:05.310
- That's amazing 'cause a lot of people

00:05:05.310 --> 00:05:07.460
give up on that dream because they can't,

00:05:07.460 --> 00:05:09.860
like I couldn't have gone if I hadn't been

00:05:09.860 --> 00:05:11.270
able to get financial
help 'cause of singing.

00:05:11.270 --> 00:05:12.960
Like that's how I had to
get scholarships and stuff.

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:14.340
- If it wasn't for sports
I wouldn't have been

00:05:14.340 --> 00:05:15.974
able to go to college
either without scholarships.

00:05:15.974 --> 00:05:16.823
You know?

00:05:16.823 --> 00:05:18.110
- Yeah, you think you
could've gotten a sports

00:05:18.110 --> 00:05:19.040
scholarship there Dave?

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:19.996
What uh?

00:05:19.996 --> 00:05:21.612
- Kelly, I'm not on trial here.

00:05:21.612 --> 00:05:23.398
(laughing)

00:05:23.398 --> 00:05:25.820
- Listen, I'm still baffled
you got a singing scholarship.

00:05:25.820 --> 00:05:26.653
Is that something?

00:05:26.653 --> 00:05:27.940
- Well I got, yeah, oh yeah!

00:05:27.940 --> 00:05:29.950
I sang opera and stuff
so I got, I could've,

00:05:29.950 --> 00:05:31.460
but then I ended up trying out for Idol.

00:05:31.460 --> 00:05:32.360
So it worked.

00:05:32.360 --> 00:05:33.625
My mom was not happy.

00:05:33.625 --> 00:05:34.458
- Ah. You flushed it all down the toilet.

00:05:34.458 --> 00:05:35.980
- Well my mom was not
happy that I was like

00:05:35.980 --> 00:05:38.950
so instead of taking advantage of this

00:05:38.950 --> 00:05:40.170
scholarship I worked hard to earn,

00:05:40.170 --> 00:05:42.390
I'm gonna move to L.A.
and live on a mattress

00:05:42.390 --> 00:05:43.545
and try and be a singer!

00:05:43.545 --> 00:05:45.026
(laughing)

00:05:45.026 --> 00:05:46.438
- Oh. And then what happened?

00:05:46.438 --> 00:05:48.180
- Well I,
(laughing)

00:05:48.180 --> 00:05:50.970
no, actually what's
funny is I worked here,

00:05:50.970 --> 00:05:53.530
'cause such a hard time,
my place burned down,

00:05:53.530 --> 00:05:54.850
lived in my car for two days,

00:05:54.850 --> 00:05:57.120
drove back home, auditioned for Idol.

00:05:57.120 --> 00:05:59.359
So yeah, You know what?

00:05:59.359 --> 00:06:00.640
Maybe go to college.

00:06:00.640 --> 00:06:01.627
(applauding)

00:06:01.627 --> 00:06:02.570
- Good job.

00:06:02.570 --> 00:06:05.370
- All right well Calais
doesn't know this, but

00:06:05.370 --> 00:06:07.980
we have a woman here
who says he completely

00:06:07.980 --> 00:06:10.270
changed the course of her life forever.

00:06:10.270 --> 00:06:12.520
Seven years ago she'd
almost given up on her

00:06:12.520 --> 00:06:13.820
dreams to go to college.

00:06:13.820 --> 00:06:15.810
She just didn't think she could afford it.

00:06:15.810 --> 00:06:17.640
That is until she met Calais.

00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:20.640
Now not only is she a college graduate,

00:06:20.640 --> 00:06:23.300
she's researching ways to cure HIV.

00:06:23.300 --> 00:06:24.832
Please welcome Deanna, ya'll!

00:06:24.832 --> 00:06:26.228
(cheering)

00:06:26.228 --> 00:06:29.844
(uplifting music)

00:06:29.844 --> 00:06:32.360
- Gosh, I love your whole look!

00:06:32.360 --> 00:06:33.822
You are so cute!

00:06:33.822 --> 00:06:35.054
Oh my gosh!

00:06:35.054 --> 00:06:37.887
(uplifting music)

00:06:43.414 --> 00:06:45.290
- Have a seat, have a seat!

00:06:45.290 --> 00:06:47.910
She's like move over, that's my seat.

00:06:47.910 --> 00:06:50.310
So Deanna what stopped you from going to

00:06:50.310 --> 00:06:51.390
college right after high school?

00:06:51.390 --> 00:06:53.940
- The biggest thing that
stopped me was financials.

00:06:53.940 --> 00:06:56.520
I was a middle child, I
was pretty good at school,

00:06:56.520 --> 00:06:58.870
I loved studying the
sciences, but most people

00:06:58.870 --> 00:07:00.670
around me either they tried to pursue

00:07:00.670 --> 00:07:02.560
higher education and
didn't finish, or they

00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:03.900
didn't have the money in the first place.

00:07:03.900 --> 00:07:04.750
So they wouldn't even go.

00:07:04.750 --> 00:07:08.630
So it kind of was out of
my range of possibility.

00:07:08.630 --> 00:07:10.570
- Yeah, and that happens
to a lot of people.

00:07:10.570 --> 00:07:11.730
College is expensive.

00:07:11.730 --> 00:07:12.810
- Yes.

00:07:12.810 --> 00:07:15.010
- But how did you land this scholarship?

00:07:15.010 --> 00:07:15.843
How did that happen?

00:07:15.843 --> 00:07:16.676
- Fate.

00:07:16.676 --> 00:07:18.810
Stars aligned. (laughs)

00:07:18.810 --> 00:07:19.643
- They're in your favor

00:07:19.643 --> 00:07:22.530
- Yeah, a mutual friend of
my family introduced me to

00:07:22.530 --> 00:07:25.690
his sister Keann and the moment
I met her we like clicked.

00:07:25.690 --> 00:07:27.820
And we did a lot of things
like we went to Costco

00:07:27.820 --> 00:07:29.680
this one time and bought a bunch of food,

00:07:29.680 --> 00:07:31.470
handed it out to the homeless downtown.

00:07:31.470 --> 00:07:32.303
- [Kelly] That's cool!

00:07:32.303 --> 00:07:33.680
- We had a bunch of charity events that

00:07:33.680 --> 00:07:35.290
we did all around Arizona.

00:07:35.290 --> 00:07:37.980
So one day her and
Calais invite me to this

00:07:37.980 --> 00:07:40.130
fancy schmancy restaurant, and I swear,

00:07:40.130 --> 00:07:41.770
I walked in and I was the only kid.

00:07:41.770 --> 00:07:43.760
All these people had 401k's and families,

00:07:43.760 --> 00:07:45.310
and I'm sitting there
sipping on apple juice

00:07:45.310 --> 00:07:47.230
in the corner trying not to be noticed.

00:07:47.230 --> 00:07:49.277
And I hear my name, they're like,

00:07:49.277 --> 00:07:50.430
"Deanna, come to the stage."

00:07:50.430 --> 00:07:52.870
And I was just like;
"Okay, what's going on?"

00:07:52.870 --> 00:07:55.040
And they go, "You got a
scholarship to go to college."

00:07:55.040 --> 00:07:56.740
And Calais goes, "Congratulations!"

00:07:56.740 --> 00:07:59.580
And I'm like: "What just happened?"

00:07:59.580 --> 00:08:00.690
- [Kelly] You had no idea.

00:08:00.690 --> 00:08:01.523
- [Deanna] Oh no it might be because

00:08:01.523 --> 00:08:02.470
I was just a child, but--

00:08:02.470 --> 00:08:04.879
- [Kelly] Best surprise
ever. That's amazing!

00:08:04.879 --> 00:08:05.712
- It was amazing.

00:08:05.712 --> 00:08:06.545
- 'Cause I mean,

00:08:06.545 --> 00:08:09.000
I feel like so many people
just need that opportunity

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:10.460
and so many people are suppressed,

00:08:10.460 --> 00:08:13.080
because you can't fly, you
know, if somebody's cutting

00:08:13.080 --> 00:08:14.620
off your wings you don't
even have the opportunity.

00:08:14.620 --> 00:08:15.911
So that's amazing!

00:08:15.911 --> 00:08:16.744
- Absolutely.

00:08:16.744 --> 00:08:18.080
- So how did the scholarship and college,

00:08:18.080 --> 00:08:19.450
how do you feel like
that changed your life?

00:08:19.450 --> 00:08:21.940
- For me, college was the foundation

00:08:21.940 --> 00:08:23.500
for who I am as a person.

00:08:23.500 --> 00:08:25.528
So I ended up going to
High Point University in

00:08:25.528 --> 00:08:28.810
High Point, North Carolina,
and I majored in biology.

00:08:28.810 --> 00:08:30.700
And the greatest thing
was not worrying about

00:08:30.700 --> 00:08:32.810
financials I was able to study harder,

00:08:32.810 --> 00:08:36.050
I was able to do outreach,
volunteering, music.

00:08:36.050 --> 00:08:39.030
Just expanding all of my horizons because

00:08:39.030 --> 00:08:40.821
I had less things to worry about.

00:08:40.821 --> 00:08:43.130
So all those research
experiences eventually

00:08:43.130 --> 00:08:45.060
led me to where I am now, which I'm a

00:08:45.060 --> 00:08:47.320
Senior Research Support Specialist at

00:08:47.320 --> 00:08:49.028
SUNY Upstate Medical University.

00:08:49.028 --> 00:08:49.954
(Cheering)

00:08:49.954 --> 00:08:50.787
- Amazing!

00:08:52.452 --> 00:08:54.810
And I love this story because look at

00:08:54.810 --> 00:08:56.570
what we would have missed out on.

00:08:56.570 --> 00:08:58.750
Like she just couldn't
get in the building,

00:08:58.750 --> 00:08:59.583
you know what I'm saying?

00:08:59.583 --> 00:09:00.610
And like, we would have missed out.

00:09:00.610 --> 00:09:02.270
And you might end up
being one of the people

00:09:02.270 --> 00:09:03.740
to like really change the trajectory of

00:09:03.740 --> 00:09:05.083
people's health and lives and that's like,

00:09:05.083 --> 00:09:06.700
it just drives me insane

00:09:06.700 --> 00:09:08.170
how expensive college is but whatever.

00:09:08.170 --> 00:09:09.483
Different show, different time, whatever.

00:09:09.483 --> 00:09:10.360
(laughing)

00:09:10.360 --> 00:09:11.310
Deanna, you have something you

00:09:11.310 --> 00:09:12.720
wanted to say to Calais right?

00:09:12.720 --> 00:09:13.553
- [Deanna] Yes.

00:09:13.553 --> 00:09:14.386
- Go ahead.

00:09:14.386 --> 00:09:15.219
- Thank you.

00:09:15.219 --> 00:09:16.430
I know that there's a
lot of ways that you can

00:09:16.430 --> 00:09:18.610
say that, but honestly without you, and

00:09:18.610 --> 00:09:20.240
the generosity of your family,

00:09:20.240 --> 00:09:22.560
and, you know, the Foundation,
I would not have been

00:09:22.560 --> 00:09:25.760
able to be who I am
today and the best thing

00:09:25.760 --> 00:09:27.840
I hope I can do in the
future is that, you know,

00:09:27.840 --> 00:09:29.429
I can open the door that you opened for me

00:09:29.429 --> 00:09:32.370
for someone who can't even get
close enough to knock on it.

00:09:32.370 --> 00:09:33.257
So, thank you.

00:09:33.257 --> 00:09:35.674
(applauding)

00:09:38.391 --> 00:09:40.648
- How's that make you feel man?

00:09:40.648 --> 00:09:41.686
You're like changing lives!

00:09:41.686 --> 00:09:42.519
- Incredible.

00:09:42.519 --> 00:09:43.352
I've got chills down my back.

00:09:43.352 --> 00:09:45.760
Like I mean, when you
give back you just hope

00:09:45.760 --> 00:09:47.690
that somebody's like
listening or somebody can

00:09:47.690 --> 00:09:50.500
take advantage of it and
like, you just don't know.

00:09:50.500 --> 00:09:52.664
You just try to do so
much and then like sitting

00:09:52.664 --> 00:09:56.534
here and hearing her speak
like this, this is incredible.

00:09:56.534 --> 00:09:57.720
It makes it all worth it.

00:09:57.720 --> 00:09:58.980
- You're absolutely right too though.

00:09:58.980 --> 00:10:00.810
Plenty of people are given opportunity,

00:10:00.810 --> 00:10:02.600
but not everybody takes advantage of it,

00:10:02.600 --> 00:10:04.120
and not everybody really runs with it,

00:10:04.120 --> 00:10:07.330
and that is a shining
example of your character.

00:10:07.330 --> 00:10:08.163
- Thank you.

00:10:08.163 --> 00:10:09.350
- And, can I just comment on your hair?

00:10:09.350 --> 00:10:11.644
When you turned sideways
I was like: "Okay!"

00:10:11.644 --> 00:10:14.330
Your hair is so awesome right now!

00:10:14.330 --> 00:10:16.080
- Thank you.
- [Kelly] Oh my gosh.

